1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,070
Can a Transformer learn to render a 3D scene
into an image?

2
00:00:05,430 --> 00:00:13,010
RenderFormer takes a scene made of triangles and
predicts an image with lighting and shadows.

3
00:00:13,270 --> 00:00:17,690
Its name may be unfamiliar, but it starts with
the attention mechanism used in language models.

4
00:00:17,930 --> 00:00:25,650
First, we will see how Transformers exchange
information, then follow how that structure

5
00:00:25,650 --> 00:00:26,630
extends to triangles and cameras.

6
00:00:26,630 --> 00:00:32,490
Rather than memorizing every equation, focus on
what goes in and what comes out.

7
00:00:33,667 --> 00:00:35,787
This video has two main parts.

8
00:00:36,027 --> 00:00:44,287
We will begin with embeddings, then cover position
information, self-attention, encoders, and decoders.

9
00:00:44,487 --> 00:00:49,227
Next, we will examine what changes when the
inputs are triangles instead of words.

10
00:00:49,267 --> 00:00:55,407
The key is a two-stage process: organize the scene,
then form an image for a particular camera view.

11
00:00:55,587 --> 00:01:01,347
Finally, we will review the training scenes, results,
and conditions this approach still struggles with.

12
00:01:02,583 --> 00:01:08,363
The Transformer was introduced in the 2017 paper
Attention Is All You Need.

13
00:01:08,603 --> 00:01:14,003
At the time, recurrent neural networks commonly
passed a previous state to the next step.

14
00:01:14,243 --> 00:01:20,143
Transformers instead centered on attention to
directly compute relationships between positions.

15
00:01:20,363 --> 00:01:23,883
This made it easier to process many positions in
parallel during training.

16
00:01:24,203 --> 00:01:28,583
The emphasis on attention refers to the main
mechanism relating tokens to one another.

17
00:01:28,583 --> 00:01:33,983
It does not mean the linear layers, feed-forward
networks, or normalization are absent.

18
00:01:35,500 --> 00:01:41,220
The encoder on the left turns input words into
vectors informed by their surrounding context.

19
00:01:41,260 --> 00:01:46,240
The decoder on the right uses that information
to predict the next output token.

20
00:01:46,340 --> 00:01:51,800
In translation, for example, the encoder reads the
source while the decoder builds the translation.

21
00:01:51,820 --> 00:01:56,020
The original base model stacked six layers on
each side.

22
00:01:56,060 --> 00:02:01,040
Repeating a structure does not mean all the
layers share one set of weights.

23
00:02:01,040 --> 00:02:06,000
For now, distinguish understanding the input from
producing an output using that understanding.

24
00:02:07,467 --> 00:02:13,307
Neural networks cannot calculate directly with letters,
so text is split into tokens and assigned IDs.

25
00:02:13,527 --> 00:02:18,307
We use whole words for simplicity here, but real
tokens can also be parts of words.

26
00:02:18,307 --> 00:02:23,347
Assigning IDs to How, are, and you turns the
sentence into a sequence of integers.

27
00:02:23,507 --> 00:02:29,647
Special tokens indicate the start, the end, and
padding used to match lengths.

28
00:02:30,027 --> 00:02:32,867
A larger ID does not mean a more important word.

29
00:02:33,027 --> 00:02:36,947
Think of the ID as an address used to find an
entry in a vocabulary.

30
00:02:38,067 --> 00:02:44,187
This small training example learns to answer How
are you with I am fine.

31
00:02:44,367 --> 00:02:46,847
The encoder receives the question's token IDs.

32
00:02:46,967 --> 00:02:51,667
The decoder receives a start token and the target
sentence shifted one position to the right.

33
00:02:52,127 --> 00:02:58,907
It predicts I from the start token, am after I,
and fine after I am.

34
00:02:58,907 --> 00:03:03,067
During training, the known beginning of the
correct answer can be supplied as input.

35
00:03:03,327 --> 00:03:07,687
During generation, the model instead uses the
tokens it has already produced.

36
00:03:08,833 --> 00:03:12,313
Token IDs alone do not readily express
relationships between words.

37
00:03:12,553 --> 00:03:16,033
Each ID is therefore mapped to a vector of real
numbers.

38
00:03:16,113 --> 00:03:17,633
This is an embedding.

39
00:03:17,793 --> 00:03:21,793
People do not manually design every word's
numerical meaning in advance.

40
00:03:21,913 --> 00:03:26,913
Useful vector representations are adjusted as
the model learns its task.

41
00:03:27,053 --> 00:03:33,193
From here on, each row is one token; the numbers
in that row are its features.

42
00:03:34,317 --> 00:03:38,817
Suppose our vocabulary has 11 entries, with six
numbers representing each token.

43
00:03:38,817 --> 00:03:45,317
For How, are, and you, we retrieve the
corresponding three rows of the embedding table.

44
00:03:45,557 --> 00:03:47,057
The result has three rows and six columns.

45
00:03:47,057 --> 00:03:51,917
We are not combining all 11 words into a single
vector.

46
00:03:52,177 --> 00:03:58,597
Real models use much wider features. The
original base Transformer used 512 dimensions.

47
00:03:58,717 --> 00:04:02,597
We use small matrices here to make the
computation easier to follow.

48
00:04:03,717 --> 00:04:07,237
The same words can mean something different when
their order changes.

49
00:04:07,317 --> 00:04:10,857
A dog bit a person and a person bit a dog are
very different stories.

50
00:04:11,217 --> 00:04:17,157
Computing relationships from token content alone does
not automatically provide sufficient order information.

51
00:04:17,297 --> 00:04:20,817
We must also tell the model where each word
appears.

52
00:04:20,937 --> 00:04:23,377
That is the role of positional encoding.

53
00:04:23,477 --> 00:04:27,757
We prepare information about both content and
position.

54
00:04:28,883 --> 00:04:33,863
The original Transformer represented positions
with sine and cosine waves of different periods.

55
00:04:33,983 --> 00:04:39,543
Positions 0, 1, 2, and so on are used to
calculate their position vectors.

56
00:04:39,663 --> 00:04:45,523
Even feature dimensions use sine, paired with
cosine in the odd dimensions.

57
00:04:45,723 --> 00:04:50,323
Different periods express a single position at
multiple scales.

58
00:04:50,443 --> 00:04:52,803
You do not need to memorize every number in the
equation.

59
00:04:52,803 --> 00:04:59,963
The idea is to turn one position index into a
numerical pattern useful for learning relationships.

60
00:05:01,083 --> 00:05:04,683
Word embeddings and positional encodings have
matching dimensions.

61
00:05:04,843 --> 00:05:07,723
Their corresponding numbers can therefore be
added.

62
00:05:07,923 --> 00:05:10,983
The number of rows and feature dimensions stays
unchanged.

63
00:05:11,223 --> 00:05:16,803
Each token now carries information about both
its identity and its position.

64
00:05:17,003 --> 00:05:19,343
This vector becomes the input to the next
attention layer.

65
00:05:19,343 --> 00:05:26,243
Addition alone does not establish meaning; later
layers learn relationships from this representation.

66
00:05:27,367 --> 00:05:32,607
Self-attention lets tokens within one sequence
refer to one another.

67
00:05:32,827 --> 00:05:38,647
To interpret a word, it computes which other
expressions should be considered with it.

68
00:05:38,887 --> 00:05:43,067
A token can receive information from several
tokens with different weights.

69
00:05:43,387 --> 00:05:48,107
A vector initially representing only a word
becomes a context-aware vector.

70
00:05:48,287 --> 00:05:52,267
Later, we will see cross-attention, which refers
to a different sequence.

71
00:05:52,267 --> 00:05:58,687
For now, remember that self means the
information comes from within the same sequence.

72
00:05:59,800 --> 00:06:04,500
One criterion may not capture several kinds of
relationships at the same time.

73
00:06:04,680 --> 00:06:07,420
We therefore use several attention heads in
parallel.

74
00:06:07,660 --> 00:06:10,600
It is like reading the same sentence from
different perspectives.

75
00:06:10,780 --> 00:06:16,840
But people do not assign fixed jobs such as
grammar to one head and emotion to another.

76
00:06:17,060 --> 00:06:22,640
Each head has its own learned projections and
discovers useful relationships during training.

77
00:06:22,900 --> 00:06:27,580
We will first follow one head, then see how the
results are combined.

78
00:06:28,800 --> 00:06:34,020
Different weight matrices transform the same
input into Q, K, and V.

79
00:06:34,320 --> 00:06:41,560
Q describes what information to seek, K provides
matching features, and V contains

80
00:06:41,560 --> 00:06:42,220
the information to pass along.

81
00:06:42,440 --> 00:06:46,320
A query, search features, and content to
retrieve are useful analogies.

82
00:06:46,460 --> 00:06:49,820
But we do not manually write three separate
sentences as inputs.

83
00:06:49,960 --> 00:06:54,280
These are all vectors calculated from the input,
using learned transformations.

84
00:06:54,400 --> 00:06:58,840
Selecting information and gathering information
use different representations.

85
00:07:00,117 --> 00:07:04,417
Relationships computed from initial weights may
not yet help solve the task.

86
00:07:04,637 --> 00:07:09,017
The model gradually changes its weights to reduce
the difference between predictions and targets.

87
00:07:09,217 --> 00:07:13,617
Repeated updates can produce representations
that make better use of context.

88
00:07:13,857 --> 00:07:17,797
This does not mean a trained model understands
every sentence perfectly.

89
00:07:17,997 --> 00:07:21,257
Its training data and capabilities still impose
limits.

90
00:07:21,397 --> 00:07:27,777
Attention diagrams offer clues about how
information is combined, but should not be treated

91
00:07:27,777 --> 00:07:28,657
as identical to human understanding.

92
00:07:29,833 --> 00:07:31,553
Now let us follow the computation.

93
00:07:31,613 --> 00:07:35,213
First, Q and K produce scores used to determine
attention weights.

94
00:07:35,273 --> 00:07:39,413
Those scores are processed, then multiplied by V
to gather information.

95
00:07:39,613 --> 00:07:45,933
Q and K determine how much to attend to; V
supplies the content being retrieved.

96
00:07:46,073 --> 00:07:49,773
All three come from the same input, but serve
different computational roles.

97
00:07:49,813 --> 00:07:54,353
Keeping this distinction in mind makes the
sequence of matrices easier to follow.

98
00:07:55,467 --> 00:07:58,907
One row of Q corresponds to one token seeking
information.

99
00:07:59,067 --> 00:08:02,687
Its dot product with each key vector produces a
score.

100
00:08:02,847 --> 00:08:06,907
We multiply by the transposed K matrix to
compute many tokens at once.

101
00:08:07,027 --> 00:08:12,487
With three tokens each attending to three
tokens, the score matrix is three by three.

102
00:08:12,607 --> 00:08:15,627
These numbers are not yet probabilities or
colors.

103
00:08:15,747 --> 00:08:21,027
They are unnormalized scores measuring
compatibility in a learned feature space.

104
00:08:22,150 --> 00:08:25,090
Let us read the resulting matrix one row at a
time.

105
00:08:25,430 --> 00:08:30,050
The first row contains the first token's scores
for attending to each token.

106
00:08:30,450 --> 00:08:35,030
The second row is from the second token's
perspective, and the third from the third token's.

107
00:08:35,030 --> 00:08:41,450
The score from A to B need not equal the score
from B to A.

108
00:08:41,890 --> 00:08:44,910
That is because Q and K are produced by
different transformations.

109
00:08:45,230 --> 00:08:49,250
Even within one sentence, different tokens may
need different information.

110
00:08:50,500 --> 00:08:53,740
As vector dimensions increase, dot-product
scores can grow larger.

111
00:08:53,840 --> 00:08:59,700
Large scores can make softmax overly
concentrated and training more difficult.

112
00:08:59,900 --> 00:09:03,520
We therefore divide by the square root of the
key dimension.

113
00:09:03,640 --> 00:09:07,060
This example has six dimensions, so we divide by
the square root of six.

114
00:09:07,220 --> 00:09:10,280
This is not a distance between objects in space.

115
00:09:10,420 --> 00:09:14,540
It is a scale factor controlling score magnitude
for the attention feature dimension.

116
00:09:15,667 --> 00:09:18,247
Next, we apply softmax to each row.

117
00:09:18,327 --> 00:09:23,407
This produces attention weights for each token;
each row sums to one.

118
00:09:23,667 --> 00:09:27,247
Relatively higher scores receive more weight.

119
00:09:27,507 --> 00:09:34,587
Rather than an objective probability of
correlation between words, these are

120
00:09:34,587 --> 00:09:35,447
the model's weights for combining information.

121
00:09:35,747 --> 00:09:40,207
Ordinary encoder self-attention does not use a
causal mask to hide future tokens.

122
00:09:40,367 --> 00:09:44,607
It can still use a mask to exclude padding added
to match sequence lengths.

123
00:09:45,867 --> 00:09:50,767
Multiplying the attention weights by V gathers
the information each token attends to.

124
00:09:50,987 --> 00:09:55,507
A token given more weight contributes more of
its value vector to the result.

125
00:09:55,747 --> 00:09:58,927
The result is a new feature vector for each
token.

126
00:09:59,187 --> 00:10:03,387
It now reflects context retrieved from other
tokens in the same sentence.

127
00:10:03,647 --> 00:10:08,147
The attention-weight matrix itself is not the
final output.

128
00:10:08,567 --> 00:10:12,227
The gathered content is what passes to the next
operation.

129
00:10:13,350 --> 00:10:15,510
So far, we have examined a single head.

130
00:10:15,610 --> 00:10:20,810
Multi-head attention performs these calculations
in several feature spaces in parallel.

131
00:10:21,110 --> 00:10:23,350
The original base model used eight heads.

132
00:10:23,350 --> 00:10:29,670
Dividing 512 dimensions among eight heads gives
each head 64 dimensions.

133
00:10:29,890 --> 00:10:34,110
These numbers are model settings, not universal
rules for Transformers.

134
00:10:34,350 --> 00:10:38,130
RenderFormer uses different dimensions and a
different number of heads.

135
00:10:39,317 --> 00:10:43,137
For simplicity, this diagram shows only two
heads.

136
00:10:43,257 --> 00:10:50,257
Each head projects the input, computes Q-K scores
and softmax, then takes a weighted sum of V.

137
00:10:50,377 --> 00:10:54,797
Implementations may project everything with one
large linear layer before splitting into heads.

138
00:10:54,977 --> 00:11:00,457
This does not split the sentence into isolated
first and second halves.

139
00:11:00,517 --> 00:11:04,077
Each head sees the same tokens through different
feature representations.

140
00:11:05,200 --> 00:11:09,440
After the heads finish, each token has several
result vectors.

141
00:11:09,640 --> 00:11:14,200
Different head weights can yield different
results for the same token.

142
00:11:14,440 --> 00:11:18,100
These are multiple perspectives that have not
yet been combined.

143
00:11:18,340 --> 00:11:21,000
Next, we concatenate them along the feature
dimension.

144
00:11:21,220 --> 00:11:26,680
We are gathering each token's features, not
increasing the number of tokens.

145
00:11:27,800 --> 00:11:31,820
After concatenation, we multiply by an output
weight matrix.

146
00:11:31,820 --> 00:11:37,920
This layer combines information from the heads
into a representation for the next layer.

147
00:11:38,200 --> 00:11:42,420
The total feature width matches the input to
allow a residual connection.

148
00:11:42,720 --> 00:11:45,400
That completes the multi-head attention block.

149
00:11:45,520 --> 00:11:50,880
Compute relationship scores, gather information,
and mix the different perspectives.

150
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:56,100
Instead of passing only the new result forward,
we add the original input as well.

151
00:11:56,240 --> 00:11:57,920
This is a residual connection.

152
00:11:58,040 --> 00:12:02,880
Think of it as adding this layer's update to the
original representation.

153
00:12:03,120 --> 00:12:08,180
It provides a direct path for information and
gradients, helping deep models train.

154
00:12:08,400 --> 00:12:14,600
Addition does not preserve all information forever
or automatically solve every training problem.

155
00:12:14,740 --> 00:12:17,800
Next comes normalization to manage the scale of
the values.

156
00:12:18,917 --> 00:12:22,897
The important question is which dimension we use
to calculate the mean and variance.

157
00:12:23,037 --> 00:12:28,357
Transformer layer normalization typically
operates across one token's feature dimensions.

158
00:12:28,557 --> 00:12:33,097
With tokens arranged in rows as shown, it uses
the numbers within each row.

159
00:12:33,237 --> 00:12:40,537
It subtracts the mean, scales using variance and a small
stability constant, then applies learned scale and bias.

160
00:12:40,957 --> 00:12:46,257
Distinguish this from batch normalization; it is
not one common mean across sentences or all tokens.

161
00:12:46,257 --> 00:12:50,797
Normalization also does not turn the data into a
normal distribution.

162
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:56,900
After attention gathers information across
tokens, we process each token's features.

163
00:12:57,180 --> 00:13:04,020
The original Transformer's feed-forward network
places ReLU between two linear layers.

164
00:13:04,160 --> 00:13:08,740
ReLU sets negative inputs to zero and passes
positive inputs through.

165
00:13:09,040 --> 00:13:13,860
This nonlinearity allows representations beyond
a simple linear transformation.

166
00:13:14,220 --> 00:13:18,840
Within a layer, the same feed-forward function
is applied to every token position.

167
00:13:19,967 --> 00:13:25,287
After the first layer and activation, the
features enter a second linear layer.

168
00:13:25,467 --> 00:13:29,747
It projects the expanded features back to the
model's base dimension.

169
00:13:30,027 --> 00:13:35,867
The original base model expands 512 dimensions
to 2,048, then returns to 512.

170
00:13:36,027 --> 00:13:39,247
The screen illustrates the same idea with small
matrices.

171
00:13:39,367 --> 00:13:43,607
Matching the output size also permits the
residual connection we saw earlier.

172
00:13:44,733 --> 00:13:49,333
Similar matrix operations repeat, but the two
blocks have distinct roles.

173
00:13:49,493 --> 00:13:53,073
Attention determines how much information to
gather from other tokens.

174
00:13:53,273 --> 00:13:58,873
The feed-forward network processes that
information nonlinearly at each token position.

175
00:13:58,953 --> 00:14:03,373
Information exchange between tokens alternates
with feature transformations within each token.

176
00:14:03,533 --> 00:14:07,173
Stacking these processes across layers gradually
changes the representations.

177
00:14:08,283 --> 00:14:12,403
Residual connection and normalization also
follow the feed-forward network.

178
00:14:12,563 --> 00:14:18,923
One encoder layer consists of attention, add and
norm, feed-forward, then add and norm again.

179
00:14:19,043 --> 00:14:23,603
Passing through these layers gives each token a
new, context-aware representation.

180
00:14:23,843 --> 00:14:30,763
The original paper normalizes after each
sublayer; many later models normalize before it.

181
00:14:30,883 --> 00:14:33,703
We will keep this distinction in mind when
looking at RenderFormer.

182
00:14:33,703 --> 00:14:38,723
Now let us see how the decoder uses the
encoder's information.

183
00:14:39,833 --> 00:14:44,133
The decoder also starts by embedding tokens and
adding position information.

184
00:14:44,293 --> 00:14:48,793
Its input is the beginning of the output
sentence, not the encoder's source sentence.

185
00:14:48,953 --> 00:14:55,593
In training, start, I, and am are used to
predict I, am, and fine, respectively.

186
00:14:55,713 --> 00:14:59,713
This is why the inputs and targets were shifted
by one position.

187
00:14:59,873 --> 00:15:03,553
The model learns to predict what comes next from
what has appeared so far.

188
00:15:04,667 --> 00:15:10,127
The decoder likewise projects its current inputs
into Q, K, and V.

189
00:15:10,487 --> 00:15:16,147
The overall process of scoring and gathering
information is the same as in self-attention.

190
00:15:16,467 --> 00:15:20,247
But sequential output generation adds a
condition.

191
00:15:20,507 --> 00:15:24,367
The current position must not see future target
tokens.

192
00:15:24,627 --> 00:15:28,687
A mask therefore restricts the positions this
attention can access.

193
00:15:30,150 --> 00:15:32,770
First, multiply Q by the transpose of K.

194
00:15:32,930 --> 00:15:36,490
Then scale the scores according to the key
feature dimension.

195
00:15:36,610 --> 00:15:39,150
This computation is the same as in the encoder.

196
00:15:39,250 --> 00:15:43,110
The next step hides scores at positions that are
not allowed.

197
00:15:43,290 --> 00:15:50,510
The math is not entirely different; the same
calculation follows different information-flow rules.

198
00:15:51,633 --> 00:15:56,133
Knowing the target sentence during training does
not mean we should reveal the next answer.

199
00:15:56,253 --> 00:16:00,413
Otherwise, the model could learn to rely on
information unavailable during generation.

200
00:16:00,593 --> 00:16:04,473
The current position must only refer to the
output prefix provided so far.

201
00:16:04,693 --> 00:16:08,313
We enforce this by blocking attention to future
positions.

202
00:16:08,473 --> 00:16:11,993
This incorporates the conditions of generation
into the training computation.

203
00:16:13,117 --> 00:16:16,177
In a matrix, the visible region forms a
triangle.

204
00:16:16,597 --> 00:16:19,417
The first position can see only the first input.

205
00:16:19,837 --> 00:16:23,337
The second can see the first and second inputs.

206
00:16:23,777 --> 00:16:26,557
Later positions can refer to increasingly long
prefixes.

207
00:16:26,837 --> 00:16:34,077
Because decoder inputs are shifted, seeing the
current input does not reveal the next target.

208
00:16:34,377 --> 00:16:36,537
This is called a causal mask.

209
00:16:37,883 --> 00:16:42,663
In code, prohibited scores are set to negative
infinity before softmax.

210
00:16:42,843 --> 00:16:46,243
Softmax turns those entries into zero weights.

211
00:16:46,323 --> 00:16:50,643
Their information is therefore excluded from the
weighted sum of V.

212
00:16:50,843 --> 00:16:54,783
Weights over the allowed positions still sum to
one.

213
00:16:54,943 --> 00:17:00,403
A mask does not create content; it restricts
where information can flow.

214
00:17:01,517 --> 00:17:04,277
Multiply the masked attention weights by V.

215
00:17:04,397 --> 00:17:09,817
The first position receives only the first
input, because all later positions are hidden.

216
00:17:10,117 --> 00:17:13,597
An entry with zero weight contributes none of
its value vector.

217
00:17:13,737 --> 00:17:17,997
Future tokens therefore cannot directly flow
into the resulting representation.

218
00:17:18,077 --> 00:17:23,077
Separating scoring from the weighted sum of
content makes the mask's role clear.

219
00:17:24,200 --> 00:17:26,900
Now consider the second and third positions.

220
00:17:26,900 --> 00:17:31,100
They combine information from their allowed
prefixes.

221
00:17:31,580 --> 00:17:34,620
Each output therefore reflects a different
amount of context.

222
00:17:34,620 --> 00:17:38,580
During training, these positions can be
calculated together as matrices.

223
00:17:39,100 --> 00:17:45,080
During generation, earlier outputs are needed as later
inputs, so tokens are usually produced sequentially.

224
00:17:45,460 --> 00:17:49,260
Remember that parallel training and sequential
generation are different things.

225
00:17:50,550 --> 00:17:55,030
Combining heads works the same way even when a
mask is used.

226
00:17:55,190 --> 00:17:59,210
Concatenate the head outputs, then apply the
output linear layer.

227
00:17:59,410 --> 00:18:03,530
This mixes the different views of the available
prefix.

228
00:18:03,730 --> 00:18:07,410
The six-by-six matrix here belongs to this small
illustrative example.

229
00:18:07,610 --> 00:18:11,630
Actual matrix sizes depend on the model
dimension and head configuration.

230
00:18:12,750 --> 00:18:16,230
Add the original decoder representation to this
result and normalize it.

231
00:18:16,510 --> 00:18:19,890
So far, the decoder has processed only the
output prefix.

232
00:18:20,170 --> 00:18:24,230
Translation and question answering also require
information about the original input.

233
00:18:24,530 --> 00:18:27,010
The encoder has already prepared that
information.

234
00:18:27,350 --> 00:18:30,890
We now connect the decoder so it can attend to
the encoder's output.

235
00:18:32,233 --> 00:18:36,313
In cross-attention, Q comes from a different
source than K and V.

236
00:18:36,413 --> 00:18:39,213
Q is created from the decoder's current
representation.

237
00:18:39,413 --> 00:18:42,553
K and V are created from the input processed by
the encoder.

238
00:18:42,693 --> 00:18:48,233
The output side asks a question and retrieves
useful information from the input side.

239
00:18:48,373 --> 00:18:51,113
This structure is also central to RenderFormer.

240
00:18:51,213 --> 00:18:56,273
There, camera-ray patches ask the questions and
the triangle-based scene provides information.

241
00:18:57,400 --> 00:19:02,820
Cross-attention is followed by residual connection,
normalization, and a feed-forward network.

242
00:19:03,040 --> 00:19:08,380
The decoder representation now reflects both the
output prefix and the original input context.

243
00:19:08,700 --> 00:19:12,340
The original paper uses six such decoder layers.

244
00:19:12,680 --> 00:19:16,640
What remains is to turn these vectors into
predictions of the next token.

245
00:19:16,880 --> 00:19:20,400
On the next page, we will connect this to the
training objective.

246
00:19:21,517 --> 00:19:26,337
The final linear layer converts each position's
features into one score per vocabulary entry.

247
00:19:26,517 --> 00:19:30,677
Softmax produces a probability distribution over
possible next tokens.

248
00:19:30,857 --> 00:19:35,097
During training, these predictions are compared
with target tokens to compute a loss.

249
00:19:35,277 --> 00:19:41,917
Backpropagation updates trainable parameters in
embeddings, attention, feed-forward layers, and more.

250
00:19:42,057 --> 00:19:47,057
The relationships Q and K respond to are learned
while reducing this objective.

251
00:19:47,057 --> 00:19:52,257
All those matrix operations are now connected to
a single prediction task.

252
00:19:53,483 --> 00:19:56,323
We now have the basic tools of the Transformer.

253
00:19:56,503 --> 00:20:02,803
It turns inputs into tokens, computes relationships,
and uses other input information to produce outputs.

254
00:20:02,963 --> 00:20:07,123
What if the input were a scene made of triangles
instead of a sentence?

255
00:20:07,223 --> 00:20:10,543
What if the output were image pixels instead of
words?

256
00:20:10,823 --> 00:20:14,243
RenderFormer applies this connection to 3D
rendering.

257
00:20:14,403 --> 00:20:19,843
We now move from relationships between words to
relationships between surfaces, light, and cameras.

258
00:20:20,967 --> 00:20:26,287
RenderFormer predicts images from triangle meshes,
materials, lighting, and camera information.

259
00:20:26,487 --> 00:20:33,367
A model trained on many scenes is used, so each
new scene does not require

260
00:20:33,367 --> 00:20:34,127
its own separate training process.

261
00:20:34,407 --> 00:20:39,507
It addresses global illumination effects such as shadows,
reflections, and light bouncing between surfaces.

262
00:20:39,627 --> 00:20:43,447
This does not mean input conversion or
normalization is unnecessary.

263
00:20:43,667 --> 00:20:49,147
Nor does a learned prediction guarantee
physically accurate results for every scene.

264
00:20:49,147 --> 00:20:55,907
The goal is to learn the rendering process from scene
to image, beyond a representation of just one scene.

265
00:20:57,250 --> 00:21:01,290
Translation transforms one token sequence into
another.

266
00:21:01,290 --> 00:21:07,890
RenderFormer considers transforming a sequence
of triangles into a sequence of image patches.

267
00:21:08,130 --> 00:21:11,490
This motivates the analogy of translating 3D
into 2D.

268
00:21:11,750 --> 00:21:18,050
The original NeRF learns a scene's radiance field
from multiple images to synthesize new views.

269
00:21:18,050 --> 00:21:24,190
RenderFormer starts from explicit triangle
meshes instead.

270
00:21:24,190 --> 00:21:29,690
We should not generalize that every method in
the NeRF family requires per-scene training.

271
00:21:30,967 --> 00:21:33,507
Let us connect the overall process.

272
00:21:33,567 --> 00:21:37,687
First, turn mesh, material, and emission
information into triangle tokens.

273
00:21:37,827 --> 00:21:42,087
The triangles exchange information to form scene
features.

274
00:21:42,327 --> 00:21:46,267
On the camera side, group rays pointing toward
the image into tokens.

275
00:21:46,607 --> 00:21:51,427
These ray tokens attend to the scene; a final
decoder reconstructs the image.

276
00:21:51,587 --> 00:21:57,067
It does not write pixels one by one from the
upper left, as if generating a sentence.

277
00:21:57,067 --> 00:22:01,807
It processes output-patch features together to
produce an image.

278
00:22:02,933 --> 00:22:04,613
The architecture has two stages.

279
00:22:04,733 --> 00:22:10,973
The view-independent stage models relationships between
triangles to obtain features useful for light transport.

280
00:22:11,193 --> 00:22:15,533
The view-dependent stage uses those features for
a particular camera view.

281
00:22:15,533 --> 00:22:20,953
Reflections and occlusion change with viewing
direction, making the second stage necessary.

282
00:22:20,973 --> 00:22:26,473
If scene and lighting stay unchanged, the first
stage's results may be reused across views.

283
00:22:26,473 --> 00:22:29,833
The 12 and six layers shown belong to the
original paper's base configuration.

284
00:22:29,833 --> 00:22:35,213
Do not equate each layer with one physical
bounce of light.

285
00:22:36,333 --> 00:22:39,733
Geometry alone is not enough to render a
triangle.

286
00:22:40,033 --> 00:22:46,913
We also need surface orientation, reflected colors,
roughness, and whether the surface emits light.

287
00:22:47,233 --> 00:22:49,993
These properties are converted into vectors the
model can process.

288
00:22:50,273 --> 00:22:54,213
The base model uses 768-dimensional triangle
features.

289
00:22:54,633 --> 00:23:00,993
Spatial position is especially important, but
the default RoPE path does not simply

290
00:23:00,993 --> 00:23:01,573
add a position index to each token.

291
00:23:01,893 --> 00:23:05,853
It incorporates 3D coordinates when computing
relationships between Q and K.

292
00:23:07,100 --> 00:23:11,120
Up close, the bunny mesh consists of many
triangles.

293
00:23:11,340 --> 00:23:18,020
Each has three vertex positions and can use
vertex normals to represent smooth surfaces.

294
00:23:18,200 --> 00:23:21,680
Normals describe which direction the surface
faces.

295
00:23:21,880 --> 00:23:26,560
Even the same color and material respond differently
to light when their orientation changes.

296
00:23:26,780 --> 00:23:32,620
Triangle counts and vertex counts differ, and
depend on how the mesh was simplified.

297
00:23:32,800 --> 00:23:39,000
Focus on turning a small surface element into an
input token, rather than the bunny's exact count.

298
00:23:40,183 --> 00:23:43,643
This page illustrates the patch inputs in the
public code.

299
00:23:43,823 --> 00:23:51,323
The paper describes 10 material values, while
the public code uses 13-channel patches

300
00:23:51,323 --> 00:23:52,043
that include normal-map channels.

301
00:23:52,103 --> 00:23:56,703
The channels contain diffuse color, specular color,
roughness, normal-map, and emission information.

302
00:23:56,903 --> 00:24:02,683
For a constant material, values repeat across the
patch; areas outside the triangle are masked.

303
00:24:02,783 --> 00:24:06,723
Flattening, linear projection, and normalization
map the patch to the token dimension.

304
00:24:06,723 --> 00:24:10,143
Repeating a value does not create new material
information.

305
00:24:10,143 --> 00:24:16,283
Rather than assuming the goal is merely to
increase parameter count, understand this

306
00:24:16,283 --> 00:24:16,983
as the code's spatial patch format.

307
00:24:18,300 --> 00:24:22,120
Linear projection can change magnitude, so
normalization adjusts the scale.

308
00:24:22,120 --> 00:24:27,760
RMS normalization uses the root mean square of
the features.

309
00:24:28,120 --> 00:24:31,720
Unlike the layer normalization seen earlier, it
does not subtract the mean.

310
00:24:32,160 --> 00:24:36,100
It also uses a small numerical-stability
constant and a learned scale.

311
00:24:36,220 --> 00:24:42,920
This can help stabilize training, but does not
guarantee that later computations

312
00:24:42,920 --> 00:24:43,460
use directional similarity alone.

313
00:24:43,620 --> 00:24:47,060
For now, think of it as controlling the
numerical scale of token features.

314
00:24:48,333 --> 00:24:51,973
Surface normals are separately converted into
vectors.

315
00:24:52,193 --> 00:24:59,253
Three vertex normals, each with x, y, and z
components, provide nine values.

316
00:24:59,433 --> 00:25:04,493
The public code can encode them with sine and
cosine at multiple frequencies.

317
00:25:04,653 --> 00:25:08,473
A linear layer projects the result to the token
dimension, followed by normalization.

318
00:25:08,533 --> 00:25:13,253
Positions describe where a surface is; normals
describe which way it faces.

319
00:25:13,413 --> 00:25:17,573
Both look like 3D vectors, but their meanings
and processing paths differ.

320
00:25:18,683 --> 00:25:23,323
Material and normal embeddings have matching
dimensions and can be added element by element.

321
00:25:23,583 --> 00:25:28,063
A learned vector shared by all triangles is also
added.

322
00:25:28,223 --> 00:25:31,423
The code calls this vector tri_token.

323
00:25:31,523 --> 00:25:39,023
Material and normal embeddings carry triangle-specific
information; the shared vector provides a learned offset.

324
00:25:39,023 --> 00:25:45,763
Calling it triangle-type information is a useful
analogy, not a fixed label for each internal dimension.

325
00:25:45,763 --> 00:25:50,723
In the default RoPE path, positions are passed
to attention separately from this sum.

326
00:25:51,833 --> 00:25:54,353
We must distinguish the branches in the code.

327
00:25:54,553 --> 00:26:01,953
Projecting a positional encoding and adding it to the
triangle vector belongs to the NeRF-encoding branch.

328
00:26:02,093 --> 00:26:08,013
The default RoPE branch adds the shared triangle
vector, material embedding, and normal embedding.

329
00:26:08,373 --> 00:26:12,873
Position information later rotates Q and K
inside attention.

330
00:26:13,333 --> 00:26:19,853
Showing both formulas as always active together
would misleadingly suggest position is added twice.

331
00:26:19,853 --> 00:26:25,013
We will follow the default RoPE path when
examining the paper's core architecture.

332
00:26:26,267 --> 00:26:30,087
The tri_token declaration registers it as a
parameter.

333
00:26:30,167 --> 00:26:33,627
That means backpropagation updates it during
training.

334
00:26:33,807 --> 00:26:40,307
Its shape is one by one by feature dimension,
allowing one vector to be added across triangles.

335
00:26:40,507 --> 00:26:45,607
Sharing a value does not mean it never changes
during training.

336
00:26:45,787 --> 00:26:53,547
All triangles use the same vector in one computation,
and training updates the shared vector itself.

337
00:26:53,827 --> 00:26:56,147
Shared and fixed are different concepts.

338
00:26:57,267 --> 00:27:02,007
The public code initializes this shared vector
with normally distributed random values.

339
00:27:02,187 --> 00:27:04,367
One point needs clarification.

340
00:27:04,507 --> 00:27:11,447
It is generally incorrect to claim that zero
initialization forces identical gradients

341
00:27:11,447 --> 00:27:12,147
and makes the vector remain one-dimensional
forever.

342
00:27:12,367 --> 00:27:18,827
Different surrounding weights and inputs can produce
different gradients from equal initial values.

343
00:27:18,987 --> 00:27:25,167
Problems with symmetric initialization of all network
weights do not automatically apply to one shared offset.

344
00:27:25,167 --> 00:27:31,607
Choosing random initialization is not evidence
that every alternative must fail.

345
00:27:33,150 --> 00:27:38,110
The learned vector is not simply a numerical
label spelling out triangle.

346
00:27:38,290 --> 00:27:42,350
It is adjusted jointly to help the overall model
predict images.

347
00:27:42,590 --> 00:27:49,850
We cannot claim that dimension 12 means position
or dimension 47 means shadow.

348
00:27:50,090 --> 00:27:53,850
Information is generally distributed across many
dimensions and layers.

349
00:27:54,010 --> 00:27:58,190
Assigning roles to individual dimensions should
only be used as a hypothetical analogy.

350
00:27:58,190 --> 00:28:04,630
The code establishes its shape, sharing
behavior, and status as a trainable parameter.

351
00:28:05,900 --> 00:28:09,320
Adding a shared vector also appears in language
models.

352
00:28:09,600 --> 00:28:13,980
For example, BERT's segment embeddings indicate
which sentence segment a token belongs to.

353
00:28:13,980 --> 00:28:21,040
RenderFormer's shared triangle vector can be
compared as learned information added in common.

354
00:28:21,440 --> 00:28:26,800
That does not make their objectives, initialization
details, or learned meanings identical.

355
00:28:27,120 --> 00:28:33,820
Separate how a vector is shared from the values
it eventually learns.

356
00:28:35,133 --> 00:28:39,653
Learnable register tokens are added alongside
the triangle tokens.

357
00:28:39,773 --> 00:28:42,193
The base model has 16 of them.

358
00:28:42,293 --> 00:28:49,173
Rather than representing individual triangles, they
can provide extra space for scene-wide information.

359
00:28:49,393 --> 00:28:55,013
They are prepended along the token axis, not
added elementwise to triangle tokens.

360
00:28:55,213 --> 00:28:59,733
With N triangles, the sequence therefore has N
plus 16 tokens.

361
00:28:59,733 --> 00:29:04,773
These separate register tokens are different
from the shared tri_token vector.

362
00:29:05,900 --> 00:29:09,180
In language, a word's position within a sentence
matters.

363
00:29:09,460 --> 00:29:15,660
For a mesh, actual 3D position matters more than
its triangle's index in a file.

364
00:29:15,840 --> 00:29:19,960
Reordering a list should not change shadows and
reflections.

365
00:29:20,080 --> 00:29:26,160
RoPE rotates parts of Q and K by position so
their dot products reflect relative position.

366
00:29:26,160 --> 00:29:31,380
RenderFormer extends this idea to the
coordinates of a triangle's three vertices.

367
00:29:31,440 --> 00:29:36,540
Relationship calculations therefore incorporate
where the token's content lies in space.

368
00:29:37,783 --> 00:29:43,043
Preprocessing can align coordinate origin and
scale before positional encoding.

369
00:29:43,303 --> 00:29:50,203
The inspected mesh-normalization function subtracts the
vertex mean and divides by twice the maximum radius.

370
00:29:50,463 --> 00:29:54,703
Its maximum radius therefore becomes 0.5, not
one.

371
00:29:54,903 --> 00:29:59,803
This illustrates why we should inspect actual
formulas, not just comments or variable names.

372
00:29:59,923 --> 00:30:04,203
Each triangle's three vertex coordinates can
then be flattened into nine values.

373
00:30:04,203 --> 00:30:12,303
In the shape B by T by 9, B is batch size, T is
triangle count, and 9 contains vertex coordinates.

374
00:30:13,417 --> 00:30:17,977
Register tokens are not real triangles, so they
have no corresponding three vertices.

375
00:30:18,077 --> 00:30:22,597
They still need a position reference for spatial
positional encoding.

376
00:30:22,777 --> 00:30:27,377
The default implementation uses the scene center
calculated from valid triangle vertices.

377
00:30:27,597 --> 00:30:31,957
This position is separate from the learned
register feature vector.

378
00:30:32,137 --> 00:30:36,157
Registers can help store global information
through attention.

379
00:30:36,317 --> 00:30:43,597
But triangles also communicate directly through
global self-attention; no single register

380
00:30:43,597 --> 00:30:45,017
is guaranteed to summarize the entire scene
perfectly.

381
00:30:46,167 --> 00:30:51,007
Multiplying each coordinate by several frequencies
represents variations at different scales.

382
00:30:51,207 --> 00:30:55,887
Here, nine coordinate components are expanded
with six frequencies each.

383
00:30:56,087 --> 00:31:01,147
That gives nine times six, or 54 angle-related
values per position.

384
00:31:01,427 --> 00:31:05,807
Low and high frequencies change at different
rates as position changes.

385
00:31:05,927 --> 00:31:09,947
This lets the model use relationships at
multiple spatial scales.

386
00:31:10,207 --> 00:31:16,827
Without these frequencies, space does not become
one-dimensional; they enrich its representation.

387
00:31:17,967 --> 00:31:20,967
Let us review the dimensions in the same
diagram.

388
00:31:21,167 --> 00:31:26,367
The paper's base model has 768 dimensions and
six heads, giving 128 dimensions per head.

389
00:31:26,367 --> 00:31:32,747
Nine coordinates with six frequencies provide 54
rotation angles for feature pairs.

390
00:31:32,927 --> 00:31:36,607
Each rotation uses two components, corresponding
to 108 dimensions.

391
00:31:36,787 --> 00:31:39,927
The remaining 20 dimensions are unchanged by
this positional rotation.

392
00:31:39,987 --> 00:31:47,087
Configuration names and internal frequency counts
may be expressed differently in implementations,

393
00:31:47,087 --> 00:31:48,167
so check the actual tensor shapes too.

394
00:31:49,283 --> 00:31:52,703
Sine and cosine are used to rotate pairs of
components.

395
00:31:52,843 --> 00:31:59,063
Rotating two vectors according to their positions lets
their dot product reflect the positional difference.

396
00:31:59,243 --> 00:32:02,243
That is the key intuition for incorporating
relative position into attention.

397
00:32:02,403 --> 00:32:08,723
This rotates feature components, not the actual
mesh in front of a camera.

398
00:32:08,843 --> 00:32:14,963
Rotation preserves a pair's length, unlike
normalization that adjusts vector magnitudes.

399
00:32:14,963 --> 00:32:21,323
It also does not guarantee identical results
under every rotation of a 3D scene.

400
00:32:22,433 --> 00:32:25,893
With the inputs ready, triangles exchange
information.

401
00:32:26,193 --> 00:32:31,573
Emissive surfaces, illuminated surfaces, and
nearby reflectors can affect one another.

402
00:32:31,893 --> 00:32:36,453
Self-attention provides an information-exchange
structure for learning such relationships from data.

403
00:32:36,793 --> 00:32:41,373
One attention connection does not correspond
exactly to one physical light ray.

404
00:32:41,693 --> 00:32:45,373
The model changes its features to better predict
rendered images.

405
00:32:45,753 --> 00:32:51,953
The resulting tokens are latent, context-aware
representations, not the original material values.

406
00:32:53,200 --> 00:32:56,780
Triangle tokens are also projected into Q, K,
and V.

407
00:32:56,900 --> 00:33:04,100
Q and K determine attention weights, and a
weighted sum of V transfers features.

408
00:33:04,300 --> 00:33:07,820
The basic principle is the same as sentence
self-attention.

409
00:33:07,920 --> 00:33:13,740
The inputs, however, are triangles with materials,
normals, and spatial relationships rather than words.

410
00:33:13,900 --> 00:33:18,220
We should not read attention scores directly as
fractions of physical light energy.

411
00:33:18,220 --> 00:33:23,660
They are learned information-combination weights
used to predict physical effects.

412
00:33:24,833 --> 00:33:28,153
Let us locate the positional encoding in this
diagram.

413
00:33:28,333 --> 00:33:32,573
Q and K are rotated according to spatial
coordinates before scores are computed.

414
00:33:32,593 --> 00:33:35,373
V carries the information combined using those
scores.

415
00:33:35,533 --> 00:33:38,753
Position therefore influences the criterion used
to compare tokens.

416
00:33:39,033 --> 00:33:45,033
Nearby triangles or triangles with similar
materials do not always receive greater attention.

417
00:33:45,173 --> 00:33:48,613
A distant light source or a surface in a
reflection direction may also matter.

418
00:33:48,793 --> 00:33:53,653
Weights depend on learned features and context
as well as position.

419
00:33:54,800 --> 00:33:57,120
Let us put the steps together again.

420
00:33:57,320 --> 00:34:00,920
First, calculate Q, K, and V from each token.

421
00:34:01,060 --> 00:34:05,560
Second, rotate Q and K using position
information and compute dot-product scores.

422
00:34:05,700 --> 00:34:09,860
Third, apply scaling and softmax, then form a
weighted sum of V.

423
00:34:10,060 --> 00:34:13,980
The numbers in the diagram illustrate these
operations at a small scale.

424
00:34:14,140 --> 00:34:21,240
The real model calculates many tokens and heads in
parallel, with stabilizers such as normalization.

425
00:34:22,433 --> 00:34:27,593
After gathering information from other tokens, a
triangle adds the result to its original representation.

426
00:34:27,813 --> 00:34:31,133
This supports gradual updates across layers.

427
00:34:31,333 --> 00:34:36,313
A direct path for the original input also helps
train a deep model.

428
00:34:36,553 --> 00:34:41,413
It is not an absolute guarantee that spatial
information is preserved without loss.

429
00:34:41,553 --> 00:34:45,213
Position is also supplied repeatedly through a
separate encoding path.

430
00:34:45,393 --> 00:34:49,833
Residual connections and positional encoding
serve different purposes and work together.

431
00:34:50,950 --> 00:34:54,490
The normalization here differs from the original
Transformer described earlier.

432
00:34:54,490 --> 00:35:01,350
RenderFormer's base architecture and inspected
code use pre-norm RMS normalization.

433
00:35:01,570 --> 00:35:06,430
Input scales are adjusted before the main
attention or feed-forward operation.

434
00:35:06,610 --> 00:35:10,130
Describing this as subtracting the mean to produce
a standard normal distribution is incorrect.

435
00:35:10,130 --> 00:35:18,230
RMS normalization uses mean-square magnitude;
normalization does not make data normally distributed.

436
00:35:18,230 --> 00:35:23,470
The principles connect, but the detailed blocks
are not identical.

437
00:35:24,583 --> 00:35:29,883
After attention gathers scene information, the
feed-forward network processes each token.

438
00:35:30,103 --> 00:35:34,683
It transforms lighting-related relationships
into features useful to later layers.

439
00:35:34,903 --> 00:35:41,043
Organizing brightness and color is an analogy; individual
dimensions are not directly measurable brightness values.

440
00:35:41,343 --> 00:35:44,723
The base view-independent stage passes through
12 such layers.

441
00:35:44,963 --> 00:35:50,483
They are not assigned fixed physical roles, such
as direct light first and one bounce second.

442
00:35:50,603 --> 00:35:54,143
The full stack learns to reduce the final
prediction error.

443
00:35:55,267 --> 00:35:59,067
We should also check the feed-forward activation
used.

444
00:35:59,227 --> 00:36:04,267
The original Transformer used ReLU, while many
other models use GELU.

445
00:36:04,447 --> 00:36:07,547
RenderFormer's default, however, is SwiGLU.

446
00:36:07,707 --> 00:36:15,487
SwiGLU transforms the input along two branches and
multiplies a smoothly activated branch with the other.

447
00:36:15,607 --> 00:36:20,967
GELU, incidentally, does not randomly pass
negative values as if flipping a coin at runtime.

448
00:36:20,967 --> 00:36:27,907
We distinguish general activation examples from
the block RenderFormer actually uses.

449
00:36:29,067 --> 00:36:36,467
In summary, position-aware attention exchanges information, while
normalization, feed-forward, and residuals update features.

450
00:36:36,607 --> 00:36:42,647
Triangle and register tokens now carry features
informed by relationships throughout the scene.

451
00:36:42,887 --> 00:36:46,767
But we have not yet produced a pixel image for
display.

452
00:36:46,987 --> 00:36:50,647
We must now introduce the camera's location and
viewing direction.

453
00:36:50,827 --> 00:36:54,707
In the next stage, camera-side tokens attend to
these scene features.

454
00:36:55,833 --> 00:37:00,853
Each camera pixel corresponds to a ray pointing
into the scene.

455
00:37:01,053 --> 00:37:05,933
RenderFormer groups these rays rather than
creating a separate token for every pixel.

456
00:37:06,133 --> 00:37:11,913
In the base configuration, one group covers
eight by eight pixels, or 64 rays.

457
00:37:12,213 --> 00:37:16,733
These rays do not mean the model directly traces
multiple light bounces through the scene.

458
00:37:16,893 --> 00:37:20,533
They are inputs describing the camera's viewing
directions.

459
00:37:20,533 --> 00:37:26,193
We now have triangle tokens for the scene and
ray tokens for the view.

460
00:37:27,317 --> 00:37:31,457
In camera coordinates, all ray origins can be
placed at the origin.

461
00:37:31,637 --> 00:37:36,837
Normalize each pixel's direction, then group 64
direction vectors.

462
00:37:37,177 --> 00:37:41,397
With three components per direction, the basic
input contains 192 numbers.

463
00:37:41,597 --> 00:37:46,297
Linear projection and normalization turn these
into a 768-dimensional token.

464
00:37:46,437 --> 00:37:50,217
Each image patch gets one such ray-patch token.

465
00:37:50,557 --> 00:37:57,117
Triangles and rays are not simply combined into
one list to do the same job;

466
00:37:57,117 --> 00:37:57,937
they become different inputs to cross-attention.

467
00:37:59,117 --> 00:38:03,357
The view-dependent stage first retrieves scene
features through cross-attention.

468
00:38:03,557 --> 00:38:08,737
Self-attention between ray-patch tokens then
exchanges image-side information.

469
00:38:08,837 --> 00:38:12,157
A feed-forward network processes the result.

470
00:38:12,377 --> 00:38:16,437
Together, these operations form features needed
for the image from this camera.

471
00:38:16,497 --> 00:38:22,377
Cross-attention does not literally execute a
conventional ray-triangle intersection algorithm.

472
00:38:22,457 --> 00:38:27,997
Nor does adding self-attention guarantee the
removal of every patch boundary or artifact.

473
00:38:27,997 --> 00:38:32,277
We are examining how these connections can
improve prediction quality.

474
00:38:33,467 --> 00:38:37,247
Recall the encoder-decoder cross-attention from
earlier.

475
00:38:37,567 --> 00:38:40,327
Here, ray-patch representations produce Q.

476
00:38:40,667 --> 00:38:45,767
Triangle and register features from the view-
independent stage provide K and V.

477
00:38:46,107 --> 00:38:49,247
The camera side queries the scene information it
needs.

478
00:38:49,587 --> 00:38:55,507
V contains learned features, not a lookup table
of finished pixel colors.

479
00:38:55,967 --> 00:39:01,467
Further processing and the final decoder are
needed to produce actual image values.

480
00:39:02,700 --> 00:39:07,600
Scene information retrieved by cross-attention is
added to the original ray-side representation.

481
00:39:07,800 --> 00:39:13,980
This accumulates information from the scene on
top of the camera-direction representation.

482
00:39:14,140 --> 00:39:17,400
It follows the same residual-update intuition
introduced earlier.

483
00:39:17,600 --> 00:39:25,340
Cross-attention itself depends on Q, so its result
is not entirely devoid of camera information.

484
00:39:25,380 --> 00:39:31,540
The residual path provides a stable way to use both
the original representation and the new update.

485
00:39:32,667 --> 00:39:36,307
The sequence exchanging information now consists
of ray patches, not triangles.

486
00:39:36,607 --> 00:39:41,967
Each patch attends to other patches, and the
feed-forward network processes the result.

487
00:39:42,347 --> 00:39:46,607
The attention operation is familiar, but the
sequence being processed has changed.

488
00:39:46,967 --> 00:39:51,207
The paper's base view-dependent stage stacks six
such blocks.

489
00:39:51,747 --> 00:39:56,967
Unlike a language decoder, it does not hide
future words and generate pixels sequentially.

490
00:39:57,487 --> 00:40:01,607
It prepares patch features together to
reconstruct the full image.

491
00:40:02,867 --> 00:40:05,667
Ray-patch tokens are still feature vectors, not
a pixel image.

492
00:40:05,667 --> 00:40:14,407
The DPT decoder rearranges them into spatial grids and
combines multiple scales into a dense image output.

493
00:40:14,507 --> 00:40:18,607
The prediction target is HDR radiance, with a
wide brightness range.

494
00:40:19,047 --> 00:40:22,547
This is different from display-ready colors
restricted to zero through one.

495
00:40:22,547 --> 00:40:30,067
RenderFormer uses a logarithmic output space;
reconstruction can be followed by tone mapping for display.

496
00:40:30,067 --> 00:40:33,747
This is the final bridge from learned features
to the image we see.

497
00:40:35,067 --> 00:40:39,927
This diagram expands the conversion from a token
sequence back into image space.

498
00:40:40,167 --> 00:40:44,947
The paper takes features from the last four
view-dependent Transformer layers.

499
00:40:45,247 --> 00:40:50,687
Each is rearranged into a 2D patch grid and
transformed to different spatial sizes.

500
00:40:50,847 --> 00:40:55,707
This prepares information at multiple resolutions
instead of using only one feature scale.

501
00:40:56,347 --> 00:41:02,607
Rather than memorizing every channel count,
notice the sequence regaining a spatial layout.

502
00:41:02,607 --> 00:41:06,847
Next, features at different sizes are combined.

503
00:41:07,983 --> 00:41:13,583
Fusion blocks progressively combine different
feature scales and restore output resolution.

504
00:41:13,663 --> 00:41:17,203
This process also uses convolutions and
interpolation.

505
00:41:17,403 --> 00:41:23,403
A Transformer-based renderer does not mean the
entire pipeline contains no convolutions.

506
00:41:23,443 --> 00:41:30,263
The inspected default HDR path does not use
sigmoid to restrict output to zero through one.

507
00:41:30,263 --> 00:41:38,043
An inverse logarithmic transform restores radiance;
separate tone mapping prepares it for display.

508
00:41:38,383 --> 00:41:43,683
Distinguish physical brightness prediction from display
color representation when interpreting the results.

509
00:41:44,983 --> 00:41:49,923
Diverse synthetic training scenes enable the
model to learn lighting relationships.

510
00:41:50,143 --> 00:41:56,643
Objects are placed in floor-and-wall templates
while materials, lights, and cameras are varied.

511
00:41:56,803 --> 00:42:02,063
Adding a wall changes not only shadows but also
light reflected onto other surfaces.

512
00:42:02,283 --> 00:42:06,803
The model learns by comparing its predictions
with images from a reference renderer.

513
00:42:06,983 --> 00:42:13,583
Log-space comparisons keep bright highlights from dominating,
alongside losses measuring perceptual differences.

514
00:42:13,583 --> 00:42:20,583
Not retraining for each new scene is entirely
different from needing no training data at all.

515
00:42:21,733 --> 00:42:29,333
Look beyond the overall impression to shadow edges,
reflected shapes, and color bleeding between surfaces.

516
00:42:29,493 --> 00:42:34,373
The reference is the comparison rendering; the
middle image is RenderFormer's prediction.

517
00:42:34,653 --> 00:42:38,713
The difference image amplifies error by a factor
of five for visibility.

518
00:42:38,973 --> 00:42:43,653
Darker areas indicate smaller differences; bright
regions show where the two results disagree.

519
00:42:43,653 --> 00:42:50,713
Generally, higher PSNR and SSIM are better,
while lower LPIPS and FLIP are better.

520
00:42:50,713 --> 00:42:56,053
These metrics measure different properties, so
examine the images as well.

521
00:42:56,253 --> 00:43:03,433
The results demonstrate learned global illumination
effects, not perfect correctness for every scene.

522
00:43:03,433 --> 00:43:10,093
Use the variations below to observe responses to
changes in geometry, lighting, and materials.

523
00:43:11,217 --> 00:43:15,597
Finally, let us summarize limitations within the
scope of the original 2025 paper.

524
00:43:15,737 --> 00:43:19,317
Attention becomes substantially more expensive
as triangle counts increase.

525
00:43:19,577 --> 00:43:25,557
Quality may fall outside the mesh sizes, materials,
lights, and camera conditions seen during training.

526
00:43:25,797 --> 00:43:31,857
Results resembling global illumination do not guarantee
every physical law or perfect accuracy in every scene.

527
00:43:31,917 --> 00:43:37,597
This research is not yet a finished general-
purpose renderer ready for every game scene.

528
00:43:37,597 --> 00:43:44,377
Still, treating triangle-based scene-to-image rendering
as one learnable pipeline is an intriguing achievement.

529
00:43:44,617 --> 00:43:51,057
Recall our progression: a structure that tokenizes
words and computes relationships became one

530
00:43:51,057 --> 00:43:53,557
that exchanges triangle features and retrieves
information for a camera.

531
00:43:53,717 --> 00:43:59,837
The input names changed, but the question remained:
how should information be represented and connected?

532
00:43:59,837 --> 00:44:06,977
Understanding Transformers and RenderFormer
together in this way can make the architectures

533
00:44:06,977 --> 00:44:07,977
of other neural-rendering studies much easier to
follow.
